// Practice quiz: Forms and HTML5 validation
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/forms-and-html5-validation

::o1 linking a label::A label reads for\="phone" and the field next to it has id\="tel". What does a screen reader announce as the field's name? {
=No name from that label, because for must equal the field's id #Correct. A label links to the control whose id equals its for value; here nothing matches, so the label names nothing.
~Phone, because the label is right next to the field #Not quite. Position on screen does not link a label; only a matching for and id, or wrapping, does.
~Tel, because the id becomes the name #Not quite. An id is an identifier for code, not a name that is read out.
~Phone, because browsers guess the nearest label #Not quite. Browsers do not guess; an unlinked label names nothing.
}

::o1 placeholder only::A visible search field has placeholder\="Search products" and no label. What is the best fix? {
=Add a visible label linked to the field #Correct. A visible control needs a visible label; the placeholder disappears as soon as someone types.
~Nothing, the placeholder already names the field #Not quite. A placeholder is a hint, not a label, and it vanishes while typing.
~Make the placeholder text longer #Not quite. A longer hint still disappears and still is not a label.
~Add a title attribute with the same text #Not quite. A title is a tooltip; a visible field still needs a visible label.
}

::o2 min on a text field::A field has type\="text" min\="18". A user types 12. What does the browser do when the form is submitted? {
=It accepts 12, because min does not apply to a text field #Correct. min and max apply to number and date types; on a text field they are ignored.
~It blocks 12, because 12 is below 18 #Not quite. That happens only with type number, where min applies.
~It changes the value to 18 #Not quite. The browser does not rewrite what a user typed on submission.
~It blocks the form because min is not allowed #Not quite. The attribute is simply ignored on a text field.
}

::o2 empty and pattern::A field has pattern\="[0-9]\{4\}" and no required attribute. The user leaves it empty and submits. What happens? {
=The form is sent, because pattern does not check an empty value #Correct. The pattern check runs only when the value is not empty; add required to block an empty value.
~The form is blocked, because an empty value does not match four digits #Not quite. An empty value skips the pattern check entirely.
~The browser fills in 0000 #Not quite. The browser never invents a value.
~The pattern makes the field required automatically #Not quite. Only the required attribute makes a field required.
}

::o3 why the server checks::A form uses required and type\="email" on every field. Why must the server still validate the data? {
=Because anyone can remove the attributes or send a request without using the page #Correct. Browser checks run on the user's computer; they help honest users but protect nothing.
~Because browsers do not support type email #Not quite. Current browsers do check type email; the problem is that the check can be bypassed.
~Because the server must re-read the HTML attributes #Not quite. The server receives only the submitted values, never the page's attributes.
~It does not need to; browser validation is enough #Not quite. A hand-made request skips the browser entirely.
}
